chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave ... The Hilbert--Poincaré series of the Chow rings of the toric varieties of matroids, often called the \\emph{Chow polynomials}, and the $f$-polynomial of their boundary complexes, called the \\emph{chain polynomials}, have been conjectured to only have real zeros.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n## Chow polynomials and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave\n\nGergely Bérczi, Basile Coron, Christopher Eur, Shiyue Li\n\nPublished: 2026-10-12 | Updated: 2026-10-14\n\nComments: v1; comments welcome!\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nmath.AG math.CO\n\nassociahedra chain polynomial chow polynomial chow ring graph log-concave matroid poincare series toric variety variety polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe Hilbert--Poincaré series of the Chow rings of the toric varieties of matroids, often called the \\emph{Chow polynomials}, and the $f$-polynomial of their boundary complexes, called the \\emph{chain polynomials}, have been conjectured to only have real zeros. We construct several explicit infinite families of matroids from finite geometries, whose multichain counts fail to be log-concave. They share a common geometric construction called \\emph{hyperplane coalescence}, together with several classical operations in projective geometry and combinatorial designs. This induces failures of log-concavity for Chow, augmented Chow, chain, and $h$-polynomials of several explicit infinite families of matroids, disproving the real-rootedness conjectures of Ferroni--Schröter, Huh--Stevens, and Athanasiadis--Kalampogia-Evangelinou from the lowest possible ranks. As an application of the same mechanism, we also give counterexamples to the log-concavity of $h$-polynomials of graph associahedra, answering in the negative a question of Sack.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2-ELONGATED PLANE PARTITIONS AND POWERS OF 7          