ordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Renato Renner ∗ ... We propose a new measure for conditional mutual information, called the reduced intrinsic informa- ... In the context of information-theoretic secret key agreement from common information [6], it is a central\nA New Measure for Conditional Mutual Information\nand its Properties\nRenato Renner ∗\nJuraj Skripsky †\nStefan Wolf ‡\nAbstract\nWe propose a new measure for conditional mutual information, called the reduced intrinsic informa-\ntion. Given a tripartite probability distribution PXY Z, the new measure is an upper bound on the\nrate S(X; Y ||Z) at which two parties, knowing realizations of X and Y , respectively, can generate\nsecret key bits unknown to a third party having access to Z. Moreover, the new bound on S is\nstrictly stronger than the best bound known previously, namely the intrinsic information shared\nby the parties. In fact, we show that since the new measure takes into account a greater class of\npotential adversarial strategies for minimizing the secret correlation between the legitimate partners,\nit can be smaller than the old bound by an arbitrarily large factor.\n1\nThe Reduced Intrinsic Information\nIn the context of information-theoretic secret key agreement from common information [6], it is a central\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nchow polynomial — arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/chow%20polynomial)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... We prove chain formulas, unimodality and gamma-positivity results, formulas under standard poset operations, and deletion formulas for matroids. ... In this paper, we formulate and prove matroid analogues of these conjectures in the setting of Chow polynomials of matroids with building sets.\n\nchow polynomial chow ring chow ring of a matroid cohomology matroid uniform matroid polynomial\n\n2026-05-27\n\nGiovanni Caiolo, Luis Ferroni, Elena Hoster\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nDual Chow polynomials of matroids and posets\n\nWe introduce and study dual Chow functions associated to kernels in incidence algebras of weakly ranked posets. Given a kernel, its dual Chow function is defined as the Chow function associated to the sign-twisted reverse kernel. For kernels satisfying a natural skew-symmetry condition, such as the Eulerian kernel of an Eulerian poset or the kernel given by R-polynomials on Bruhat intervals, this construction recovers the ordinary Chow function. In contrast, when this skew-symmetry fails, the dual Chow function gives a genuinely different invariant. The main example considered in this paper is the dual Chow function associated to the characteristic function. We develop the basic theory of these dual Chow functions, with particular emphasis on posets arising from matroids. We prove chain formulas, unimodality and gamma-positivity results, formulas under standard poset operations, and deletion formulas for matroids. Along the way, we also obtain a general deletion formula for the ab-index of matroids, which leads to new formulas for extended ab-indices and, in turn, specializes to several deletion formulas appearing in the literature.\n\n\nacyclic orientation chow polynomial convex polytope graded orientation polytope poset polynomial partition\n\n2026-04-06\n\nBasile Coron, Luis Ferroni, Shiyue Li\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nMatroid analogues of Gal's conjecture\n\nWell-known conjectures of Charney--Davis, Gal, and Nevo--Petersen predict increasingly strong positivity phenomena for the h-vectors of flag simplicial spheres. In this paper, we formulate and prove matroid analogues of these conjectures in the setting of Chow polynomials of matroids with building sets. Our proofs rely on toric geometry and make crucial use of tropical intersection theory. We begin by introducing complete building sets, a class encompassing all maximal building sets and other important families such as minimal building sets of braid matroids. For matroids with complete building sets, we analyze the Chow rings of the associated toric varieties, and prove that their Hilbert--Poincaré polynomials are gamma-positive. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRyan PEPPER | Professor, Department Chair | PhD | University of Houston - Downtown, Houston | UHD | Department of Mathematics and Statistics | Research profile (https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Ryan-Pepper-2)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; We show that for any connected graph G, α(G)≥C(G) 2+1, where α(G) is the independence number of G and C(G) is the number of cut-vertices of G. ... The independent-domination number of a graph is the cardinality of a smallest set of mutually non-adjacent vertices which has the property that every vertex not in the set is adjacent to at least one that is.\nView\n\nIndependence Number and Cut-Vertices\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jan 2011\n\nCongressus Numerantium\n\n  * Ryan Pepper\n\nWe show that for any connected graph G, α(G)≥C(G) 2+1, where α(G) is the independence number of G and C(G) is the number of cut-vertices of G. The bound is sharp and the case of equality is characterized. This inequality was conjectured by the computer program Graffiti during classroom use.\n\nView\n\nGraffiti.pc on the independent-domination number of a graph\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jan 2011\n\nCongressus Numerantium\n\n  * E. De la Viña\n  * Ryan Pepper\n  * B. Waller\n\nThe independent-domination number of a graph is the cardinality of a smallest set of mutually non-adjacent vertices which has the property that every vertex not in the set is adjacent to at least one that is. We present several conjectures made by the computer program Graffiti.pc about the independent-domination number of graphs, providing proofs a...\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChow polynomials and chain polynomials of matroids are not log-concave · PaperPeel (https://www.paperpeel.com/paper/2610.17135)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; For Chow polynomials and the augmented Chow polynomials, their real-rootedness for uniform matroids were first proved [23, Theorem 1.10] and by Brändén and Vecchi [8, Theorem 1.1]. ... About 5 hours and 12 hours later, SL found using Claude Opus 5.5 three explicit families of matroids as counterexamples to both the log-concavity of chain polynomials and Chow polynomials of lowest ranks possible, which are presented here; soon after, GB also found using ChatGPT Astra a different family of counterexamples to the log-concavity of Chow polynomials.\nBy Newton’s inequalities, the three real-rootedness conjectures are false. ∎\n\nThe key mechanism which allows us to transfer failures of log-concavity of multichain counts to other polynomial invariants, is given by Theorem A. We show that a similar mechanism also applies to the study of the so-called graph associahedra and their h-polynomials.\n\nRecall that for a connected graph G, its associated graph associahedron [11] is the simple polytope whose dual simplicial complex is the simplicial complex of tubings, which are compatible families of nonempty proper connected vertex sets. Its h-polynomial is denoted by h G​(t)=∑j​h j​(G)t j. We refer to [41] for more details of graph associahedra and its connections to Boolean matroids with building sets. For several prominent families of graphs, these polynomials and their positivity properties have been studied under different names. For instance, the h-polynomials of complete graphs coincide with the Eulerian polynomials, for path graphs we get Narayana polynomials, for cycle graphs we obtain type B Narayana polynomials and finally, the h-polynomials of star graphs correspond to binomial-Eulerian polynomials. All these polynomials are known to be real-rooted using various techniques with wide range of connections to analysis, partial differential equations, topology and geometry (see [28, pp. 829–830] for Eulerian polynomials, [9, Corollary 7.2] for type A and type B Narayana polynomials, and [30, Theorem 3.1] for binomial-Eulerian polynomials). However, we show in Section 5 that h-polynomials of graph associahedra are not necessarily log-concave, and hence not real-rooted.\n\n\n### AI disclosure\n\nLLMs were substantially used in the searches of these counterexamples. In an upcoming update of this paper, we will release a detailed analysis on the search history from an AI-methodology point of view. A brief summary of the searches is as follows.\n\nThe third author CE found a non-constructive disproof of the real-rootedness conjecture for Chow polynomials using ChatGPT Astra on September 23, and communicated to the fourth author SL about the nature of the disproof. Upon suggestions by SL, Matt Larson, and Lorenzo Vecchi, disproofs of the real-rootedness of augmented Chow and chain polynomials were obtained similarly on September 29. These disproofs involved inexplicit matroids of rank at least 2 65, whose existence relies on probabilistic methods, with no claim to the failure of log-concavity.\n\nPrompted by the lack of concrete and explicit counterexamples of low ranks, and the question of whether log-concavity can fail, on September 30, the second author BC found using ChatGPT Astra a counterexample to the log-concavity of h-polynomials of graph associahedra (a special case of built matroids) of very high vertex count. On October 4, the first author GB, BC, and SL then orchestrated and launched an LLM-search to target specifically the failure of log-concavity for both the chain polynomials and the Chow polynomials. About 5 hours and 12 hours later, SL found using Claude Opus 5.5 three explicit families of matroids as counterexamples to both the log-concavity of chain polynomials and Chow polynomials of lowest ranks possible, which are presented here; soon after, GB also found using ChatGPT Astra a different family of counterexamples to the log-concavity of Chow polynomials.\n\nGenerative LLMs were further used for drafting the reports for the initial findings, literature search, identifying common patterns among the examples such as hyperplane coalescence, and producing figures. The mathematical content was digested and reshaped from the LLM search reports, checked, and written by the authors.\n\n### Acknowledgements\n\nWe thank the organizers of the BIRS-Oaxaca Workshop on “Algebra and Geometry of Matroids” from September 27 to October 2, 2026 for the wonderful conference, where CE communicated to SL about his LLM-generated disproof of real-rootedness and sparked the efforts on disproving log-concavity. We also thank Luis Ferroni for suggesting the consideration of multichain counts in Theorem A, which simplifies our proofs, as well as Matt Larson and Lorenzo Vecchi for helpful conversations. The question about h-polynomials of graph associahedra was raised to the fourth author SL by Andrew Sack, and so we thank him for his communication and interest. SL is supported by a Donald J. Lewis postdoctoral fellowship at the University of Michigan. CE is supported by US NSF DMS-2246518 and Sloan Fellowship and GB is supported by DNRF 42296.\n\n### Organization\n\nSection 2 reviews the polynomial invariants and proves Theorem A. Section 3 develops hyperplane coalescence and absorption, and studies the changes of the multichain counts under coalescence. Section 4 constructs the four geometric families, proves Theorem B in Section 4.4, and derives the 3-connected rank-7 counterexamples of Theorem C in Section 4.5.\n\n## 2 2. Polynomial invariants and multichain counts\n\nIn this section, we review basic properties of the relevant polynomial invariants of matroids, and prove Theorem A. We then develop the parallel amplification argument for graph associahedra under wedges. Throughout, M is a loopless matroid of positive rank r, L denotes the lattice of flats of M, and L denotes the poset of nonempty proper flats of M.\n\n### 2.1. Chain and face enumeration polynomials\n\nf(L;t)=k⩾0∑​f k−1​(L)t k,f(L;t)=k=0∑r−1​f k−1​(L)t k.\n\nf k−1​(L)=S⊆[r−1]∣S∣=k​∑​α M​(S).\n\n### 2.2. Multichain counts\n\nZ M​(m):=#{0=F 0​⩽F 1​⩽⋯⩽F m​=1}(m⩾0).\n\nf 1​(L)2−f 0​(L)f 2​(L)=Z M​(3)2−Z M​(2)Z M​(4).\n\nZ M⊕n​(m)=Z M​(m)n.\n\nZ M​(j)2<Z M​(j−1)Z M​(j+1)\n\nZ M⊕n​(j−1)Z M⊕n​(j+1)Z M⊕n​(j)2​=(Z M​(j−1)Z M​(j+1)Z M​(j)2​)n<1.\n\n### 2.3. Chow polynomials and augmented Chow polynomials\n\n### 2.4. Asymptotic coefficients\n\nIn this section, we give asymptotic formulas for the coefficients of the aforementioned polynomial invariants of the direct sum M⊕n in terms of the multichain counts of M. \n### 2.5. Face enumerations of graph associahedra\n\nWe now establish the graph analogue of Proposition 2.6. For matroids, direct sums give products of lattices of flats. For rooted graphs, wedge of graphs gives products of posets of connected vertex sets containing the root, which will play an analogous role.\n\nThe construction of a graph with non log-concave h-polynomial has two distinct steps. First, we find a rooted connected graph whose numbers of multichains of rooted connected sets fail log-concavity. Then identify the roots of sufficiently many copies of that graph. We prove that this operation transfers the failure of log-concavity of the multichain counts to the h-polynomial of the resulting graph associahedron, and we will construct the explicit rooted graph in Section 5.\n\n### 2.5.1. Tubings and height functions\n\nh G​(t)=T∑​t∣T∣(1−t)n−1−∣T∣,\n\nAs recalled in Section 2.6, Newton’s inequalities imply that a real-rooted polynomial with nonnegative coefficients is log-concave. \n## 3 3. Matroid operations: hyperplane coalescence, absorption\n\nWe study the properties of two crucial matroid operations, which are essential in our constr